ent the weekly scan files carries `evidence/scan-finding.json`, not `detection.json`; `finding.read_any` maps it to metric `security: <class>`, rule `scan`, tier 3, and there are no failed-run URLs. Add one requirement: `detected` is read through `finding.read_any`, the `detection` source points at whichever file exists, `run-<n>` sources are absent for a scan incident, and the class tag is the scan's `class` field (item 1).\n10. Determinism the build would otherwise guess, write each down: the index line is `* [title](file) - description. Tier n, Mon yyyy.` (the proposal's); \"newest first\" sorts by `detected.at` descending, then file name; `## Unsorted` comes after `## Retired`; `build` with zero lessons writes the heading and one line \"No lessons yet.\"; the gate finds the lesson by `lessons/*-<slug>.md` (the month varies); the required fields are every field of the table except `runbook` and `supersedes`, which are optional; \"the eval case exists\" means `evals/cases/<id>-<slug>/prompt.md` and `checks.yaml` both exist. Acceptance says \"three sub-checks\" and lists five: make the count match.\n11. Restore these intent sentences the spec dropped, one Requirements bullet each: a stale lesson is still read and the maintain run says when it relied on one; `description` is one sentence in the metric's terms; a hand edit of the index is undone by the next `build`; `/sdlc-init` copies the README as today; no workflow template changes; the sample stays frozen; decision 17 unchanged.\n12. Add the `supersedes` rule to the deploy requirement: when the new lesson carries `supersedes: <file>`, step 0b sets the earlier lesson's `status: retired` and adds `retired_by: <the new file>` in the same commit; the gate (e) sub-check verifies the superseded file exists and reads `retired`.\n13. Both command files' `allowed-tools` frontmatter gains `Bash(python \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/plugin/lessons/index.py\" *)`, and the body quotes the call the same way (the instruction test requ